The Peak of MUDs               NannyMUD Times                         Page 4
by Kefka

  The Peak of MUDs... and Online Multiplayer Gaming?
  
  Hi, this is the formerly relevant wizard Kefka.  You may remember me
  from such failed projects as "The New Damned", "The New Damned, Take
  2", or "My Area".  I'm making good use of Astrodeath's invitation to
  write an article for the Times to editorialize on MMORPGs, as the
  kids call them, and how they aren't as good as they were back in the
  day.  So sit down in a semicircle around my rocking chair; there'll
  be no talking, no squirming and no disagreement.  Just listen to 
  gramps and hope he falls asleep before you do.
  
  When I started with Nanny back in '95, the average eventual World of
  Warcraft player was either slogging their way through the first
  Warcraft game, marvelling at its boring, derivative gameplay and 
  falling face-first into a slavish devotion to Blizzard, or they were
  two.  In either case, they were missing out on what I truly believe
  to be the "Golden Age" of online multi-player RPGs.  MUDs were
  springing up left and right.  Free engines abounded, so anyone with
  half a lick of programming ability and a 386 on a college network
  could set their own up.  And MUDs reaching their zenith equates to 
  MMORPGs reaching their (to date) zenith, as far as I'm concerned.
  
  From a pure playability standpoint it's hard to argue that a text 
  based game is as much fun as a graphical one.  Generally it's going
  to come down to "it improves imagination" idealistic hokum, and the 
  success of even the most primitive graphical MMORPGs proves that a 
  little flash goes a long way.  I'm not going to argue that MUDs are,
  by today's standards, better games than the cream of the modern 
  MMORPG crop.  What I am going to argue, though, is that relative to
  their time they were better.
  
  I'll be honest: most MUDs sucked.  Bandwidth and computer power were
  in such a sorry state that a single FTP site on the network could 
  wreck the former and few dozen people logged into the mud could 
  overload the latter.  Many were nothing more than a sad attempt at
  realizing the great D&D campaign their creator had thought up in 
  high school but never got around to writing.  Some were so
  unplayable that they quickly degenerated into MUCKs; ironically,
  many of these still exist and maintain a healthy, if static,
  membership.[0]
  
  Beyond these failures, though, there were amazing successes.  Given
  that the majority of MUDs were free, and text content was so easy to
  add, game size tended to expand rapidly.  Whereas most modern
  MMORPGs ship with all the content you are ever going to see, save
  for the $10 expansion that comes out in six months, the same
  timespan could see the playable area on a free MUD double.  With no
  financial incentive, MUDs didn't feel the need to base advancement
  and item acquisition curves on what was most likely to keep the
  players hooked and coughing up money.  Balance was achieved purely
  within the game world, generally on what would be the most fun for
  the greatest number of players, rather than being sullied by real-
  world financial implications, like "how do we keep the ones paying
  the most happy?"
  
  The huge difference, though, is the ease and egalitarianism of 
  content creation.  Nanny is a quintessential example: if you play 
  the game long enough and well enough, you are allowed to actually
  change it.  No modern MMORPG boasts such capability, beyond minor
  cosmetic changes.[1]  I feel that while MUDs can't compete with the
  polish of modern graphical games, the variety and creativity of 
  their content is still far beyond anything we've seen from even the
  best graphical MMORPGs.
  
  While Nanny and Co. undoubtedly claimed players' social lives in a
  similar fashion to WoW and Co., the difference is that some of those
  MUD players got something out of it.  I'm sure you can find an
  example or two of people whose WoW habit led to them learning 
  how to program.  Players learning or greatly developing their 
  programming as direct result of mudding, on the other hand, was 
  common.  Improving your writing, your typing, a second
  language... there were a bevy of real-world skills that mudding 
  honed, and that graphical MMORPGs don't.
  
  There was also a closer bond between players.  With seven million 
  people playing WoW, it's hardly a relevant enough similarity to be
  the basis of a real relationship.  Mudding, on the other hand, was
  an unusual enough pastime that two mudders meeting for the first 
  time had an instant geek-bond that would get them through the 
  awkward social debacle that would inevitably follow.  While meeting
  your significant other online has become commonplace nowadays, it 
  was happening on MUDs ten plus years ago.
  
  I think it comes down to this: while not all mudders came away from
  the glory days of MUDs with anything beyond "Holy cow, I wasted a 
  whole lot of time playing online games", many did.  Some, such as 
  myself, came away as a much stronger coder.  Several met their 
  spouses, and many more have real-life friendships that will last the
  rest of their lives.  Compare this to current MMORPGs, where the
  lament of wasted time will be joined with a lament of wasted money,
  and, for the most part, a complete lack of long-term gains.
  
  As technology develops, I'm sure good, free MMORPGs with open 
  content creation will spring up.  These will be a true heir to the
  MUD lineage, but the original magic that MUDs had will be long gone.
  Professional MMORPGs like WoW will get better and better, while 
  graphical MUCKs like Second Life will get more and more immersive.
  It wouldn't surprise me if people spending their entire waking lives
  in online games/environments becomes common within our lifetimes.
  
  At least to me, though, none of it will compare to those days when
  online games were new and exciting, players were more than slack 
  jawed teenagers and professional gold miners, anyone could have 
  their own wizard's castle, men were men, women were women, you had 
  to walk twelve miles through the snow to get to work, a loaf of 
  bread only cost 2 coins, and, and.... zzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz
  
  /Kefka
  
